representations.normalized:
Consider a polling system consisting of $N\ge 2$ queues $Q_1, \dots, Q_N$ served by a single server. Customers arrive at queue $Q_i$ according to a stochastic arrival process (e.g. Poisson with rate $\lambda_i$), require i.i.d.\ service times with finite mean, and the server incurs a switch-over time $S_{ij}$ when moving from $Q_i$ to $Q_j$. Fix the loads $\rho_i=\lambda_i\mathbb{E}[B_i]$ with total load $\rho=\sum_i\rho_i<1$. Scale the switch-over times by a factor $s>0$ (so each $S_{ij}$ is replaced by $sS_{ij}$) and let $s\to\infty$. Under the cyclic exhaustive (or cyclic gated) discipline with a fixed pre-determined visit order, it is known that, after appropriate scaling, the cycle times converge to a deterministic limit and the scaled waiting-time distribution at each queue converges to a uniform distribution on an explicit interval determined by $\rho_i$ and the mean switch-overs.

Provide a complete classification of the triples (service discipline, visit discipline, scheduling discipline) for which this same deterministic limiting behaviour holds as the switch-over times tend to infinity. The classification should cover:
(i) service disciplines beyond exhaustive and gated (e.g.\ $k$-limited, time-limited, globally gated, Bernoulli, mixed gated/exhaustive, etc.);
(ii) visit disciplines including dynamic visit orders, where the next queue to be served is not fixed a priori but is determined adaptively (e.g.\ based on queue states);
(iii) scheduling disciplines governing the order of service within a visit.

Determine necessary and sufficient conditions on (i)–(iii) under which, as the scaling parameter of the switch-over times tends to infinity, the scaled cycle time converges to a deterministic constant and the scaled waiting-time distributions at the queues converge to the corresponding uniform limiting distributions.

