representations.normalized:
Consider an N-electron quantum system with Hamiltonian H = H_0 + V_ext(t), where H_0 contains kinetic energy and pairwise Coulomb interactions and V_ext(t) is a weak, time-dependent external one-body perturbation. For p = 1, 2, ..., N, define the p-th reduced density matrix by partial trace, ρ_p(x_1,...,x_p; x'_1,...,x'_p; t) = (N!/(N-p)!) Tr_{p+1,...,N} ρ_N(t), where ρ_N(t) is the full density matrix evolving via i ∂_t ρ_N = [H, ρ_N]. The reduced density matrices satisfy the BBGKY hierarchy, in which the equation for ρ_p couples to ρ_{p+1} through the two-body interaction and is generally nonlinear when expressed via cluster (cumulant) decompositions ρ_2 = ρ_1 ∧ ρ_1 + c_2, ρ_3 = ρ_1 ∧ ρ_1 ∧ ρ_1 + 3 ρ_1 ∧ c_2 + c_3, etc.

Linearize the hierarchy around a stationary reference state ρ^{(0)}_p (e.g., the ground state) by writing ρ_p(t) = ρ^{(0)}_p + δρ_p(t) and retaining only terms of first order in δρ_p and in V_ext. The resulting equations of motion form the linear-response BBGKY hierarchy.

Develop a closed, well-posed truncation of this linear-response hierarchy at finite order p ≤ p_0 (for some small p_0, e.g. p_0 = 2 or 3) such that:

(i) the truncation defines a linear time-evolution generator L on the truncated set (δρ_1, ..., δρ_{p_0}) whose spectrum yields finite, physically meaningful neutral excitation energies (poles of the optical response);
(ii) basic conservation laws (particle number, total energy at the reference state, and, when applicable, the f-sum rule for the dipole response) are preserved;
(iii) the dynamics is free of the secular/divergent behavior known to afflict nonlinear truncations of the time-dependent BBGKY hierarchy, in the sense that ‖δρ_p(t)‖ remains bounded for all t ≥ 0 under bounded V_ext;
(iv) in suitable limits the scheme reduces to known linear-response approximations such as time-dependent Hartree–Fock (RPA) at p_0 = 1 and to second-RPA-type theories at p_0 = 2.

Construct such a truncation, prove (i)–(iv), and characterize the resulting optical absorption spectrum σ(ω) = -(ω/π) Im Tr[d̂ δρ_1(ω)] for a dipole operator d̂.

