A quandle is a set $Q$ equipped with a binary operation $*: Q\times Q\to Q$ such that (i) $a*a=a$ for all $a\in Q$, (ii) for each $b\in Q$ the map $x\mapsto x*b$ is a bijection of $Q$, and (iii) $(a*b)*c=(a*c)*(b*c)$ for all $a,b,c\in Q$. Classical (rack/quandle) homology is defined via the chain complex $C_n^R(Q)=\mathbb Z[Q^n]$ with boundary $\partial(x_1,\dots,x_n)=\sum_{i=2}^n(-1)^i\big[(x_1,\dots,\hat x_i,\dots,x_n)-(x_1*x_i,\dots,x_{i-1}*x_i,\hat x_i,\dots,x_n)\big]$, and quandle homology $H_n^Q(Q;A)$ is obtained by quotienting out degenerate subcomplexes; the associated cocycle state-sum invariants distinguish classical knots and links.

A virtual quandle is a quandle $Q$ equipped with an additional bijection $f:Q\to Q$ that is a quandle automorphism (so $f(a*b)=f(a)*f(b)$), used to color the virtual crossings of a virtual knot or link diagram while the quandle operation colors classical crossings; virtual quandle colorings are invariant under the generalized Reidemeister moves of virtual knot theory.

Problem. Construct a homology (and dual cohomology) theory $H_*^{VQ}(Q,f;A)$ for virtual quandles $(Q,f)$ that:
(a) reduces to ordinary (rack/quandle) homology when $f=\mathrm{id}_Q$;
(b) admits a state-sum cocycle invariant assigning, to each virtual knot or link diagram $D$ together with a virtual-quandle coloring, a Boltzmann weight using $H^n_{VQ}(Q,f;A)$ that is invariant under all generalized Reidemeister moves (including the virtual moves and the mixed move);
(c) determine which classes of virtual knots and links are distinguished by the resulting invariants but cannot be distinguished by classical quandle cocycle invariants applied to the underlying combinatorial data, and exhibit explicit pairs of virtual knots separated only by these new invariants.

