A virtual knot diagram is a 4-valent plane graph whose vertices are decorated either as classical crossings (with over/under information) or as virtual crossings (depicted by a small circle), considered up to generalized Reidemeister moves. To each virtual diagram D one associates an atom, namely a closed (possibly nonorientable) surface F together with an embedded 4-valent graph G ⊂ F (the underlying graph of D) whose complementary regions are 2-colored (black/white) so that opposite local regions at every vertex receive the same color; the atom genus g(D) is the genus of F. Equivalently, g(D) measures the smallest genus of a thickened surface in which the diagram can be realized as an alternating-like projection compatible with its Gauss data. For a diagram D with n classical crossings let ⟨D⟩ denote the Kauffman bracket and span⟨D⟩ its breadth; one always has span⟨D⟩ ≤ 4n − 4g(D).

Address the following:

(a) Develop effective methods that, for a given concrete virtual knot diagram D, certify that the atom genus g(D) is minimal among all virtual diagrams representing the same virtual knot.

(b) Develop effective methods that certify minimality of the classical crossing number of D, including the more general setting in which one projects the underlying knot to a closed orientable surface Σ_h of genus h ≥ 0 and seeks the minimum number of crossings of any such projection representing the given (virtual) knot type.

(c) In particular, derive lower bounds for these crossing numbers (in the plane and on higher-genus surfaces) from:
   (i) the Kauffman bracket polynomial and its span;
   (ii) Thistlethwaite-type spanning-tree expansions of the Kauffman bracket;
   (iii) atom-based invariants;
   (iv) Khovanov homology of virtual links (for example via the height/width of its bigraded support).

Determine to what extent each of these invariants yields sharp estimates, and identify classes of diagrams for which the resulting bounds prove minimality of crossing number and/or atom genus.

