A topological group $G$ is called a pro-Lie group if it is isomorphic (as a topological group) to a closed subgroup of a (possibly infinite) Cartesian product of finite-dimensional real Lie groups; equivalently, $G$ is complete and every identity neighborhood of $G$ contains a closed normal subgroup $N$ such that $G/N$ is a finite-dimensional Lie group. Recall that a topological group is Polish if its underlying topological space is separable and completely metrizable, separable if it has a countable dense subset, and compactly generated if it is generated algebraically by some compact subset.

Develop a satisfactory structure and Lie theory for the following three classes of pro-Lie groups, with particular emphasis on the connected case:

(a) Polish pro-Lie groups;
(b) separable pro-Lie groups;
(c) compactly generated pro-Lie groups.

Specifically, characterize when a pro-Lie group belongs to each of these classes in terms of its Lie algebra $\mathfrak{L}(G)$ and its quotients by suitable closed normal subgroups, establish appropriate analogues for these classes of the structure theorems known for connected pro-Lie groups (such as the Levi–Mal'cev decomposition, the existence of maximal compact subgroups, and the exponential function $\exp:\mathfrak{L}(G)\to G$), and determine to what extent classical results valid for connected locally compact groups (e.g., open mapping theorems, closed-subgroup theorems, the structure of abelian and solvable groups) extend to each of (a), (b), and (c).

