Let $\Omega\subset\mathbb{R}^4$ be a bounded domain with smooth boundary $\partial\Omega$, and let $S$ denote the best Sobolev constant for the embedding $H^1_0(\Omega)\hookrightarrow L^4(\Omega)$, i.e.
$$S=\inf_{u\in H^1_0(\Omega)\setminus\{0\}}\frac{\int_\Omega|\nabla u|^2\,dx}{\left(\int_\Omega u^4\,dx\right)^{1/2}}.$$
Fix $2<q<4$ and positive parameters $a,b,\lambda,\delta>0$. Consider the Kirchhoff-type Brezis–Nirenberg problem
$$-\Bigl(a+b\int_\Omega|\nabla u|^2\,dx\Bigr)\Delta u=\lambda|u|^{q-2}u+\delta|u|^{2}u\quad\text{in }\Omega,\qquad u=0\text{ on }\partial\Omega.\tag{$\ast$}$$

A known existence theorem asserts: if $bS^2<\delta<2bS^2$, $\Omega$ is strictly star-shaped, and one of the following holds,
\begin{itemize}
\item[(C1)] $a>0$ and $\lambda>0$ is sufficiently small;
\item[(C2)] $\lambda>0$ and $a>0$ is sufficiently large;
\item[(C3)] $a>0$, $\lambda>0$, and $\delta/b>S^2$ is sufficiently close to $S^2$;
\end{itemize}
then $(\ast)$ admits a nontrivial weak solution.

Question: Are the hypotheses $\delta<2bS^2$, the strict star-shapedness of $\Omega$, and the auxiliary parameter conditions (C1)–(C3) all necessary in order to guarantee the existence of a nontrivial solution to $(\ast)$? Equivalently, can existence of a nontrivial solution still be ensured if any of these hypotheses is dropped or weakened?

