In relativistic heavy-ion collisions, the azimuthal distribution of produced particles is characterized by Fourier flow coefficients v_n. For each event one defines v_n via dN/dφ ∝ 1 + 2 Σ_{n≥1} v_n cos(n(φ − Ψ_n)), and the two-particle cumulant flow harmonic v_n{2} is defined through the two-particle azimuthal correlator ⟨⟨cos(n(φ_1 − φ_2))⟩⟩ = v_n{2}^2, with higher-order cumulants v_n{4}, v_n{6}, … extracted from multi-particle correlators in the standard cumulant expansion. Soft physics refers to the low transverse-momentum p_T regime (p_T ≲ 2 GeV) typically modeled by relativistic hydrodynamics applied to a fluctuating initial state, while hard physics refers to the high-p_T regime governed by hard parton production and energy loss in the produced medium.

Construct a single, unified theoretical framework (with a fixed set of physical parameters, including initial-state model, transport coefficients, equation of state, and energy-loss prescription) that, in one calculation, simultaneously reproduces, within experimental uncertainties, the following observables in heavy-ion collisions at a given collision energy:
(i) the flow harmonics v_n{2}(p_T, centrality) for n = 2, 3, 4, … as functions of transverse momentum and centrality;
(ii) the higher-order cumulants v_n{4}, v_n{6}, v_n{8} and the corresponding cumulant ratios for the same n and centralities;
(iii) observables spanning both the soft (low-p_T) and hard (high-p_T) regimes over a large range in transverse momentum, including the nuclear modification factor R_AA(p_T) and high-p_T azimuthal anisotropies.

Either exhibit such a framework or prove that no such single calculation with one fixed parameter set can simultaneously match all of (i)–(iii).

