Consider relativistic heavy-ion collisions in which the azimuthal distribution of final-state particles in a single event is expanded in a Fourier series

  dN/dφ ∝ 1 + 2 Σ_{n≥1} v_n cos[ n(φ − Ψ_n) ],

where v_n ≥ 0 are the anisotropic flow harmonics and Ψ_n are the corresponding event-plane angles. Because of finite-multiplicity event-by-event fluctuations and non-flow correlations, experimentally accessible flow observables depend on the analysis method used. Standard methods include:

 (i) the scalar-product method, based on Q-vectors Q_n = Σ_j e^{i n φ_j} and yielding a quantity v_n{SP};
 (ii) two-particle correlation methods, returning v_n{2} = √⟨⟨ e^{i n(φ_1 − φ_2)} ⟩⟩;
 (iii) multi-particle cumulant methods of order 2k, returning v_n{2k} obtained from the cumulants c_n{2k} of the 2k-particle azimuthal correlations;
 (iv) event-plane methods with finite resolution R_n, returning v_n{EP}.

Each method gives, in general, a different moment or function of the underlying event-by-event probability distribution P(v_n, Ψ_n) of the flow vector V_n = v_n e^{i n Ψ_n}.

Problem. Given a theoretical model (for example a hydrodynamic or hybrid simulation) that produces, for an ensemble of events, the single-event particle distributions dN_e/dφ together with their multiplicities, formulate explicit estimators that reproduce, on the theoretical side, the observables v_n{SP}, v_n{2}, v_n{2k} (for k = 2, 3, 4), and v_n{EP} as measured experimentally — including the effects of finite multiplicity, finite event-plane resolution, and any kinematic cuts and weights used in the experimental analyses — so that the resulting theoretical numbers can be compared apples-to-apples with the corresponding experimental values. In particular, identify under what conditions on P(v_n, Ψ_n) and on the non-flow contributions these different estimators agree or differ, and quantify the leading corrections.

