representations.normalized:
Consider relativistic heavy-ion collisions modeled by an initial-state geometry with event-by-event fluctuations followed by hydrodynamic evolution. The transverse momentum spectrum of produced hadrons is characterized by its azimuthal Fourier decomposition $dN/d\phi \propto 1 + 2\sum_{n\ge 1} v_n \cos(n(\phi-\Psi_n))$, where $v_n$ are the harmonic flow coefficients and $\Psi_n$ the event-plane angles. Mean transverse-momentum fluctuations are quantified by event-by-event variations of $\langle p_T\rangle$, which are sensitive to the number $N_s$ of independent sources (e.g., participant nucleons or hot spots) entering the initial-state energy/entropy density. 

Develop theoretical calculations within a chosen initial-condition plus viscous hydrodynamic framework that:

(i) predict $\langle p_T\rangle$ event-by-event fluctuations with statistical precision sufficient to discriminate between models with different numbers of initial-state sources $N_s$;

(ii) compute higher harmonic flow coefficients $v_n$ ($n \ge 2$) at center-of-mass energies below top RHIC energies (i.e., across the RHIC beam energy scan) and for collision systems other than Au+Au and Pb+Pb, in particular Cu+Cu and U+U;

(iii) provide identified-particle (e.g., $\pi^\pm$, $K^\pm$, $p,\bar p$) flow harmonics $v_n(p_T)$ as functions of transverse momentum.

Determine whether such calculations can be carried out with sufficient statistical accuracy to meaningfully constrain $N_s$ and to extend the model–data comparison beyond top-energy collisions.

