Let $n\ge 2$. Let $\mathcal{H}^1(\mathbb{R}^n)$ denote the real Hardy space, and for a locally integrable function $b$ let $[b,T]f := bTf - T(bf)$ denote the commutator of multiplication by $b$ with an operator $T$. Write $I$ for the Riesz potential of order $1$ (so $\widehat{If}(\xi)=|\xi|^{-1}\widehat{f}(\xi)$ up to a constant), and $R_\alpha$, $\alpha=1,\dots,n$, for the Riesz transforms on $\mathbb{R}^n$.

Recall that an $\mathcal{H}^1$-atom is a function $a\in L^\infty(\mathbb{R}^n)$ supported in some ball $B$ with $\|a\|_{L^\infty}\le |B|^{-1}$ and $\int a=0$. Given $b\in L^\infty(\mathbb{R}^n)$, a $b$-atom is a standard $\mathcal{H}^1$-atom $a$ that satisfies in addition $\int a\,b\,dx=0$.

Fix a bounded Lipschitz map $H:\mathbb{R}^{n+1}\to\mathbb{R}$, fix $u\in W^{1,n}(\mathbb{R}^n,\mathbb{R}^{n+1})$, and put $b := H\circ u\in W^{1,n}(\mathbb{R}^n)\cap L^\infty(\mathbb{R}^n)$. The Jacobian $n$-form is $du^1\wedge\cdots\wedge du^n$, identified with the scalar $\det(Du)$ (or, more generally, with the collection of $n\times n$ minors of $Du$ when $u$ has $n+1$ components), which is known to lie in $\mathcal{H}^1(\mathbb{R}^n)$.

Determine:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] the largest subspace $V\subset \mathcal{H}^1(\mathbb{R}^n)$ such that $[b,I]:V\to L^{n/(n-1)}(\mathbb{R}^n)$ is bounded; the largest such $V$ if $L^{n/(n-1)}(\mathbb{R}^n)$ is replaced by $W^{1,1}(\mathbb{R}^n)$; and whether this $V$ contains all $n\times n$ minors of $Du$;
\item[(b)] the largest subspace $V\subset \mathcal{H}^1(\mathbb{R}^n)$ such that $[b,R_\alpha]:V\to L^1(\mathbb{R}^n)$ is bounded for every $\alpha=1,\dots,n$, and whether this $V$ contains all $n\times n$ minors of $Du$;
\item[(c)] whether $du^1\wedge\cdots\wedge du^n$ admits an atomic decomposition into $b$-atoms;
\item[(d)] whether the answers to (a)–(c) change under the additional assumption that $u$ solves an $H$-system, i.e.\ $\Delta u = 2\,H(u)\,u_{x_1}\wedge\cdots\wedge u_{x_n}$ in the sense of distributions.
\end{enumerate}

