Let X be a (real or complex) infinite-dimensional separable Banach space, and let B_X denote its closed unit ball, equipped with the weak topology inherited from X. Let K(B_X) denote the collection of all weakly compact subsets of B_X, partially ordered by set inclusion.

Recall that two directed partially ordered sets (P,≤_P) and (Q,≤_Q) are Tukey equivalent if there exist maps f: P → Q and g: Q → P such that f sends cofinal subsets of P to cofinal subsets of Q and g sends cofinal subsets of Q to cofinal subsets of P; equivalently, there exists a directed partial order into which both embed cofinally.

Consider the following four "standard" directed posets:
(i) the trivial one-element poset {0};
(ii) N^N, the set of functions from the natural numbers to themselves, ordered by everywhere domination f ≤ g ⇔ f(n) ≤ g(n) for all n;
(iii) K(Q), the family of compact subsets of the rationals Q (with the usual topology), ordered by inclusion;
(iv) [c]^{<ω}, the family of finite subsets of the continuum c, ordered by inclusion.

Question: Is it relatively consistent with ZFC that there exists a separable Banach space X such that the poset K(B_X) is not Tukey equivalent to any of {0}, N^N, K(Q), or [c]^{<ω}?

