Let $(\Omega,\Sigma,\mu)$ denote the Lebesgue measure space on $[0,1]$, and let $X$ be a real Banach space with topological dual $X^{*}$. A function $f:[0,1]\to X$ is called strongly measurable if it is the $\mu$-almost everywhere limit of a sequence of simple $X$-valued functions. For a strongly measurable function $f:[0,1]\to X$, set $\|f\|_{\infty}=\operatorname*{ess\,sup}_{t\in[0,1]}\|f(t)\|_{X}$, and let $L^{\infty}([0,1],X)$ be the Banach space of (equivalence classes of) strongly measurable functions with $\|f\|_{\infty}<\infty$.

A sequence $(f_n)$ of strongly measurable functions $f_n:[0,1]\to X$ is said to:
\begin{itemize}
\item converge \emph{scalarly in measure} to $0$ if, for every $x^{*}\in X^{*}$, the scalar sequence $(\langle x^{*},f_n(\cdot)\rangle)$ converges to $0$ in measure on $[0,1]$;
\item converge \emph{scalarly almost everywhere} to $0$ if, for every $x^{*}\in X^{*}$, the scalar sequence $(\langle x^{*},f_n(t)\rangle)$ converges to $0$ for $\mu$-almost every $t\in[0,1]$.
\end{itemize}

The space $X$ is said to have property $(D_{\infty})$ if every sequence $(f_n)$ of strongly measurable functions $f_n:[0,1]\to X$ that is bounded in $L^{\infty}([0,1],X)$ and converges scalarly in measure to $0$ admits a subsequence $(f_{n_k})$ converging scalarly almost everywhere to $0$.

Question: Does $X=L^{1}[0,1]$ have property $(D_{\infty})$?

