Consider a supermassive black hole (a compact massive object whose gravitational potential dominates dynamics within its sphere of influence) residing at the center of a galaxy, surrounded by a circumnuclear region of gas and stars. Using observational constraints obtained from the Galactic Center of the Milky Way and from the nuclei of nearby galaxies, characterize:

(a) the modes and rates of gas accretion onto the central black hole, including the role of tidal disruption of gas clouds;

(b) the properties of star formation in the immediate vicinity of the black hole (the circumnuclear region), including initial mass function, spatial distribution, and triggering mechanisms;

(c) the quantitative relationship between black-hole feeding (gas inflow and accretion), feedback (radiative, mechanical, and outflow-driven energy and momentum injection into the surrounding medium), and ongoing or recent circumnuclear star formation;

(d) the influence of the central compact massive object on the stellar and gaseous dynamics of the surrounding nuclear region.

Provide a unified empirical and theoretical description that explains how feeding, feedback, and star formation are coupled in the central parsec-to-hundred-parsec environment of a supermassive black hole.

