Consider the following open-shop scheduling problem. We are given m machines $M_1, \dots, M_m$ and n jobs. Each job $j \in \{1,\dots,n\}$ consists of m operations $O_{1j}, \dots, O_{mj}$, where operation $O_{ij}$ must be processed on machine $M_i$ for exactly one unit of time (i.e., $p_{ij} = 1$). Each job $j$ has a release date $r_j \in \mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}$ (no operation of job $j$ may start before time $r_j$), a due date $d_j \in \mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}$, and a nonnegative weight $w_j$. A schedule assigns to every operation $O_{ij}$ an integer start time $s_{ij} \geq r_j$ such that:
(i) on each machine, no two operations overlap in time;
(ii) for each job, no two of its operations overlap in time;
(iii) the order in which the m operations of a job are processed may be chosen freely (open-shop).
Job $j$ is on time if $\max_i (s_{ij}+1) \leq d_j$, and tardy ($U_j = 1$) otherwise. The objective is to minimize $\sum_j w_j U_j$, the weighted number of tardy jobs.

Question: Is this problem (denoted $O \mid p_{ij}=1, r_j \mid \sum w_j U_j$) fixed-parameter tractable when parameterized by the number m of machines? That is, does there exist a computable function $f$ and a constant $c$ such that the problem can be solved in time $f(m) \cdot |I|^c$, where $|I|$ is the input size?

