In general relativity, a black hole spacetime is modeled by a 4-dimensional Lorentzian manifold $(M,g)$ satisfying the Einstein field equations. While in the stationary case the boundary of a black hole is unambiguously captured by notions such as the event horizon, the Killing horizon, the apparent horizon, the trapping horizon, and the isolated horizon (which all coincide), in genuinely time-dependent situations these notions diverge and none is universally accepted as the correct mathematical model of the evolving boundary of a black hole.

Recall that on a spacelike 2-surface $S \subset M$ with outward unit spacelike normal $r^a$ and future unit timelike normal $\tau^a$ (within a chosen 3-slice), the outgoing and ingoing null normals are $\ell^a = \tau^a + r^a$ and $n^a = \tau^a - r^a$, with associated expansions $\Theta_{(\ell)}$ and $\Theta_{(n)}$. A 2-surface is marginally (outer) trapped if $\Theta_{(\ell)} = 0$ and $\Theta_{(n)} < 0$. One existing proposal defines a dynamical horizon as a smooth 3-dimensional spacelike submanifold $H \subset M$ that admits a foliation by closed marginally trapped 2-surfaces, but this notion is non-unique (foliation-dependent), can fail to be spacelike (becoming null or timelike) in dynamical regimes, and is not known to satisfy desirable quasi-local laws (e.g.\ a generalized second law) in full generality.

Problem. Formulate a mathematically precise, geometrically natural, and quasi-local definition of a "dynamical horizon" — that is, a hypersurface (or family of 2-surfaces) in a general 4-dimensional Lorentzian spacetime $(M,g)$ — such that:
(i) the definition reduces to the event/Killing/isolated-horizon notion in the stationary limit;
(ii) the resulting horizon is uniquely determined by local geometric data (independent of an arbitrary choice of foliation or slicing of spacetime);
(iii) it remains well-defined under generic time-dependent evolution, allowing matter/radiation flux across it;
(iv) it satisfies analogues of the laws of black-hole mechanics, including a monotonicity (area-increase / second-law) property under physically reasonable energy conditions; and
(v) it correctly captures the intuitive notion of the evolving boundary of a black hole in general dynamical settings.

