Consider a quantum theory of gravity in which a black hole forms from the gravitational collapse of matter initially prepared in a pure quantum state, and subsequently evaporates completely via Hawking radiation. Semiclassical analysis (using quantum field theory on a fixed curved background) predicts that the emitted Hawking radiation is in a thermal mixed state determined essentially by the black hole's mass, charge, and angular momentum, so that the final state after complete evaporation appears to be mixed. This contradicts unitary evolution in quantum mechanics, which requires a pure initial state to evolve to a pure final state and forbids loss of quantum information.

The problem is to determine, within a consistent theory that combines general relativity with quantum mechanics, whether the quantum information encoding the initial pure state of the collapsing matter is preserved throughout the formation and complete evaporation of the black hole, and to give a precise mechanism describing how this information is (or is not) recovered in the final state of the radiation. A complete resolution should specify:

(i) whether the global evolution of the formation-and-evaporation process is unitary;
(ii) how, if unitarity holds, the fine-grained entropy of the Hawking radiation evolves with time (in particular, whether it follows the Page curve, rising and then falling to zero as the black hole evaporates);
(iii) the physical mechanism by which information initially behind the horizon is transferred to, or encoded in, the outgoing radiation, in a way consistent with locality and the equivalence principle (or a precise account of which of these principles must be modified, and how).

Provide such a resolution, or prove that no resolution compatible with the standard postulates of quantum mechanics exists.

