A weighted graph is a pair $G_w = (G, w)$ consisting of a simple graph $G = (V, E)$ on $n$ vertices together with a weight function $w : E \to \mathbb{R} \setminus \{0\}$. Its (weighted) adjacency matrix $A(G_w) = (a_{ij})$ is the symmetric $n \times n$ real matrix with $a_{ij} = w(ij)$ if $ij \in E$ and $a_{ij} = 0$ otherwise. Since $A(G_w)$ is real symmetric, its eigenvalues are real; let $i_+(G_w)$, $i_-(G_w)$, and $i_0(G_w)$ denote, respectively, the number of positive, negative, and zero eigenvalues of $A(G_w)$ (counted with multiplicity), called the positive inertia index, negative inertia index, and nullity of $G_w$.

A connected graph $G$ is called bicyclic if $|E(G)| = |V(G)| + 1$, equivalently if $G$ contains exactly two independent cycles. Call $G_w$ a weighted bicyclic graph of order $n$ if $G$ is bicyclic on $n$ vertices.

Determine, up to isomorphism (of the underlying graph together with the sign pattern / structural type of the weights), all weighted bicyclic graphs $G_w$ of order $n$ satisfying each of the following three conditions:
(a) $i_+(G_w) \le 2$;
(b) $i_-(G_w) \le 2$;
(c) $i_0(G_w) \ge n - 4$.

