Let $G$ be a finitely generated group, and let $\mathrm{Diff}^{\infty}_+(S^1)$ denote the group of orientation-preserving $C^{\infty}$ diffeomorphisms of the circle, equipped with the $C^{\infty}$ topology. A homomorphism $\varphi: G \to \mathrm{Diff}^{\infty}_+(S^1)$ is called a faithful action if it is injective. A nonempty closed $\varphi(G)$-invariant subset $K \subseteq S^1$ is called minimal if every $\varphi(G)$-orbit of a point in $K$ is dense in $K$; the action is called minimal if $K = S^1$ is minimal, and $K$ is called an exceptional minimal set if it is minimal and homeomorphic to a Cantor set.

Suppose $\varphi_0: G \to \mathrm{Diff}^{\infty}_+(S^1)$ is a faithful action that admits an exceptional minimal set. Does there exist a continuous path $t \mapsto \varphi_t$, $t \in [0,1]$, in the space of faithful homomorphisms $G \to \mathrm{Diff}^{\infty}_+(S^1)$ (with respect to the $C^{\infty}$ topology), starting at the given $\varphi_0$, such that:
\begin{itemize}
\item for every $t \in [0,1)$, the action $\varphi_t$ admits an exceptional minimal set; and
\item the action $\varphi_1$ is minimal (i.e., every $\varphi_1(G)$-orbit is dense in $S^1$)?
\end{itemize}

