Consider the random-matrix-theory model of compound-nucleus scattering in which the scattering matrix S(E) is constructed from a Hamiltonian drawn from the Gaussian Orthogonal Ensemble (GOE) coupled to a finite number of open channels via fixed channel-coupling vectors. For incident energy E, write S_{ab}(E) for the channel-to-channel S-matrix elements and define the fluctuating cross sections σ_{ab}(E) ∝ |S_{ab}(E) − ⟨S_{ab}(E)⟩|^2.

The following GOE averages are known analytically:
(i) the two-point S-matrix correlator ⟨S_{ab}(E_1) S_{cd}^*(E_2)⟩ for arbitrary degree of resonance overlap;
(ii) selected three- and four-point S-matrix correlators;
(iii) the marginal probability distribution of an individual S-matrix element;
(iv) the complete joint probability distribution of all S-matrix elements, but only in the Ericson regime of strongly overlapping resonances (Γ ≫ D, where Γ is the mean resonance width and D the mean level spacing).

Problem. Derive practically usable closed-form GOE expressions for cross-section correlation functions
C_{ab,cd}(ε) = ⟨σ_{ab}(E) σ_{cd}(E+ε)⟩ − ⟨σ_{ab}(E)⟩ ⟨σ_{cd}(E+ε)⟩
that are valid outside the Ericson regime — in particular in the regime of isolated and weakly overlapping resonances (Γ ≲ D) — for arbitrary numbers of open channels and arbitrary transmission coefficients. More generally, obtain analytic expressions for higher cross-section correlations and for the complete joint distribution of S-matrix elements beyond the Ericson limit.

