Let G = (V,E) be a finite simple graph. A matching of G is a set M ⊆ E of pairwise non-adjacent edges; the induced subgraph ⟨M⟩ is the subgraph of G whose vertex set V(M) consists of all endpoints of edges in M and whose edge set is the set of all edges of G with both endpoints in V(M).

Given a graph property P (a class of graphs closed under isomorphism), call a matching M of G a P-matching if ⟨M⟩ has property P. A P-matching M is called maximal if M is not properly contained in any larger P-matching of G. Define the parameters
  β_P(G) = max{ |M| : M is a P-matching of G },
  β_P^-(G) = min{ |M| : M is a maximal P-matching of G }.

Examples of properties P that have been studied in this framework include:
 (i) P = "the induced subgraph is 1-regular" (the standard induced/strong matching);
 (ii) P = "acyclic" (matchings whose induced subgraph is a forest, i.e. uniquely restricted matchings);
 (iii) P = "connected";
 (iv) P = "disconnected";
 (v) P = "the induced subgraph has maximum degree at most k" for fixed k ≥ 1;
 and analogous variants obtained from other natural graph properties.

Problem. For each of the above choices of P, establish nontrivial upper bounds for β_P(G) and nontrivial lower bounds for β_P^-(G), expressed in terms of standard graph invariants of G (such as the number of vertices n, number of edges m, maximum degree Δ, minimum degree δ, or matching number).

