Let $F=\mathbb{F}_q$ be a finite field of order $q=p^n$ with $p$ prime, and let $\psi\colon F\to\mathbb{C}^\times$ be the canonical additive character $\psi(x)=\exp(2\pi i\,\mathrm{Tr}_{F/\mathbb{F}_p}(x)/p)$. For an integer $d$ with $\gcd(d,q-1)=1$ (an "invertible exponent" over $F$), define the Weil sum of the binomial $x^d+ax$ by
$$ W_{F,d}(a)\;=\;\sum_{x\in F}\psi(x^d+ax),\qquad a\in F. $$
The Weil spectrum of $(F,d)$ is the set
$$ \mathcal{W}_{F,d}\;=\;\{\,W_{F,d}(a)\;:\;a\in F\,\}\subseteq\mathbb{C}. $$
We say $\mathcal{W}_{F,d}$ is three-valued if $|\mathcal{W}_{F,d}|=3$.

Define an equivalence relation on pairs $(F,d)$ as follows: $(F,d)\sim(F',d')$ if and only if $F=F'=\mathbb{F}_{p^n}$ and there exists an integer $j$ with $0\le j<n$ such that $d'\equiv p^j d \pmod{q-1}$, or $d\, d'\equiv p^j\pmod{q-1}$ (i.e., $d$ and $d'$ are related by multiplication by a power of $p$ modulo $q-1$, possibly composed with multiplicative inversion modulo $q-1$). Pairs equivalent under this relation produce identical Weil spectra.

The known three-valued cases consist of the following infinite families (each up to the above equivalence):
1. $d\equiv 1\pmod{q-1}$ trivial cases excluded; the nontrivial known families include the Gold exponents $d=p^k+1$ with $n/\gcd(n,k)$ odd over $\mathbb{F}_{p^n}$;
2. the Kasami exponents $d=p^{2k}-p^k+1$ with appropriate parameters;
3. the Welch exponent $d=2^{(n-1)/2}+3$ over $\mathbb{F}_{2^n}$, $n$ odd;
4. the Niho exponents over $\mathbb{F}_{2^n}$, $n$ odd;
5. and the remaining sporadic and infinite families catalogued in the standard tables of three-valued Weil spectra.

Question: Is every pair $(F,d)$, where $F$ is a finite field and $d$ is an invertible exponent over $F$ such that the Weil spectrum $\mathcal{W}_{F,d}$ is three-valued, equivalent (under the equivalence relation above) to a pair belonging to one of the known families listed above? Equivalently, is the known classification of three-valued Weil spectra of binomials over finite fields complete?

