Consider the bootstrap algebraic multigrid (BAMG) framework for solving large sparse linear systems $A u = f$ arising from the discretization of partial differential equations on a grid or unstructured mesh. In BAMG, a multilevel hierarchy is constructed adaptively: a set of test vectors is generated by relaxation and used to define coarsening (selection of coarse degrees of freedom) and interpolation operators $P_\ell$ between successive levels, from which coarse operators $A_{\ell+1} = P_\ell^T A_\ell P_\ell$ are formed. In its standard form, the setup phase relies only on algebraic information available from the matrix $A$ and the computed test vectors.

Devise a systematic methodology that augments the bootstrap algebraic multigrid setup so that, whenever supplementary information about the underlying problem is available — in particular geometric information such as the spatial coordinates of degrees of freedom, mesh connectivity, element shape, anisotropy directions, coefficient variations, or boundary data — this information is used to:

(a) guide the selection of coarse variables (coarsening);
(b) determine the sparsity pattern and weights of the interpolation operators $P_\ell$;
(c) influence the choice of smoothers and the overall setup;

in such a way that the resulting multigrid hierarchy provably (or empirically, with quantifiable improvement) exploits the supplied geometric/problem information, yielding faster convergence and lower setup cost than the purely algebraic bootstrap procedure, while still reducing to the algebraic procedure when no extra information is provided.

