Consider the oblique, thread-like cool structures observed in solar prominences along the magnetic field lines of the large arches that emit at 304 Å (the He II resonance line, dominated by plasma at temperatures of order 10^4.5–10^5 K). These threads exhibit rapid plane-of-sky motions and apparent counter-streaming (oppositely directed flows along neighboring field lines).

Determine which of the following physical mechanisms is responsible for the observed fast motions of cool material along these oblique threads:

(a) Rapid radiative cooling and condensation of EUV-emitting (∼10^6 K) coronal plasma onto the field lines, with the resulting cool condensations sliding under gravity and pressure gradients;

(b) Large-amplitude longitudinal oscillations of the cool prominence material along dipped magnetic field lines, in which material trapped in different magnetic dips moves to one side or the other and produces an apparent counter-streaming pattern when integrated along the line of sight;

(c) Large-scale siphon flows driven by pressure differences between the two footpoints of long magnetic flux tubes, anchored in regions of unequal magnetic field strength, producing net mass transport from one end of the prominence to the other.

Identify, on the basis of observational and theoretical evidence (flow speeds, periodicities, spatial coherence, persistence, and footpoint conditions), which of (a), (b), or (c) is the dominant cause of the rapid oblique-thread flows in the large 304 Å arches, or give a quantitative criterion that decides between them.

