In the standard ΛCDM cosmological framework, gas inside dark-matter halos is expected to acquire angular momentum through tidal torques and to subsequently cool and collapse to form rotationally supported disk galaxies. In practice, fully cosmological hydrodynamical simulations have historically produced disks that are too compact and bulges that are too massive: excessive early cooling of gas into small subhalos, followed by mergers, transfers a substantial fraction of the baryonic angular momentum to the surrounding dark matter (the so-called "angular momentum catastrophe"), yielding stellar systems whose specific angular momentum is much lower than that observed in real spiral galaxies.

Formulate and demonstrate a self-consistent prescription for baryonic physics (gas cooling, star formation, and stellar/AGN feedback) within a fully cosmological hydrodynamical simulation that, starting from cosmological initial conditions in a ΛCDM universe, produces at redshift z = 0 a Milky-Way-mass galaxy (halo mass ~10^12 M☉) which is:

(i) rotationally supported, with a disk circular velocity dominating over velocity dispersion (V/σ ≫ 1) throughout the stellar disk;
(ii) endowed with a thin, extended, high–angular-momentum stellar disk whose specific angular momentum j★ matches the observed Fall–Romanowsky relation for late-type spirals;
(iii) characterized by a small bulge-to-total stellar mass ratio (B/T ≲ 0.2), consistent with observed Sb–Sc spirals;
(iv) free from the angular-momentum catastrophe, in the sense that the net transfer of specific angular momentum from baryons to dark matter during assembly is small enough to leave the disk's j★ within observational scatter.

Specify quantitatively the physical mechanisms (e.g., feedback efficiency, coupling to the interstellar medium, suppression of early star formation in low-mass progenitors) responsible for retaining baryonic angular momentum, and show that the resulting galaxy population reproduces the observed thin-disk fraction.

