Consider the standard astrophysical procedure for inferring the stellar mass of a galaxy by fitting its observed multi-band spectral energy distribution (SED) to synthetic spectra produced by a stellar population synthesis (SPS) model. The synthetic spectra are obtained by convolving stellar evolutionary tracks and stellar atmosphere libraries with an assumed initial mass function (IMF), a star formation history (SFH), a chemical-enrichment/metallicity prescription, a treatment of advanced evolutionary phases (in particular the thermally pulsing asymptotic giant branch, TP-AGB), a dust attenuation law and assumed dust–star geometry, a prescription for nebular emission lines, and possible additional physics such as stellar rotation and binary stellar evolution.

Quantify the true total systematic uncertainty (e.g. as a multiplicative factor or dex offset) on the inferred stellar mass M⋆ of an individual galaxy that arises from the joint freedom in:
(i) the choice of SPS code/model;
(ii) the treatment of the TP-AGB phase;
(iii) the assumed metallicity;
(iv) the assumed IMF (including potentially non-universal or unusual IMFs);
(v) the parametrization of the SFH;
(vi) the adopted dust attenuation law and dust geometry;
(vii) the treatment of nebular emission lines;
(viii) the inclusion of stellar rotation;
(ix) the inclusion of binary stellar evolution; and
(x) other physical ingredients that are currently missing or untested in SPS models ("unknown unknowns").

In particular, determine a defensible upper bound on the systematic error budget for SED-derived stellar masses that accounts not only for the variation among existing model choices ("known unknowns") but also for ingredients not yet incorporated or tested.

