Consider ab initio nuclear many-body calculations of the lepton–nucleus electroweak response that employ a realistic nucleon–nucleon interaction (such as the Argonne v18 potential) together with three-nucleon forces, with the nuclear ground and low-lying excited states obtained by quantum Green's Function Monte Carlo (GFMC) techniques. These calculations are intrinsically nonrelativistic and, owing to their computational cost, are presently feasible only for light nuclei (mass number A ≤ 12). For accelerator-based neutrino experiments, the charged-current quasi-elastic (CCQE) process ν_ℓ + A → ℓ^- + X involves momentum transfers |q| and energy transfers ω that can be comparable to or exceed the nucleon mass, so that relativistic kinematics and dynamics become essential.

Develop a theoretical framework that extends, or systematically connects, GFMC-type ab initio nuclear calculations of inclusive electroweak response functions to the relativistic regime of momentum and energy transfers relevant to accelerator neutrino beams, in such a way that:
(i) the high-quality treatment of nuclear correlations, two-body currents, and nuclear ground-state structure provided by GFMC is preserved;
(ii) relativistic kinematics for the struck nucleon(s) and the hadronic final state is incorporated consistently;
(iii) the resulting predictions for inclusive CCQE cross sections on nuclei of experimental interest are well-defined and can be compared with measured neutrino–nucleus data.

Specify the theoretical construction (e.g., choice of reference frame, treatment of the recoiling residual system, matching to relativistic current operators) and quantify the regime of (|q|, ω) in which the extension is justified.

