representations.normalized:
Let G be a finitely presented group with a finite generating set S, and let B_S(n) denote the number of elements of G expressible as a product of at most n elements of S ∪ S^{-1}. The growth function n ↦ B_S(n) is said to be:
- of polynomial growth if there exist constants C, d > 0 such that B_S(n) ≤ C n^d for all n ≥ 1;
- of exponential growth if there exists a constant a > 1 such that B_S(n) ≥ a^n for all sufficiently large n;
- of intermediate growth otherwise (i.e., faster than every polynomial but slower than every exponential function).

Up to the standard equivalence of growth functions, this classification does not depend on the choice of finite generating set S.

Question: Does every finitely presented group have either polynomial growth or exponential growth? Equivalently, does there exist a finitely presented group of intermediate growth?

