representations.normalized:
Consider an isolated rotating neutron star whose angular frequency $\Omega(t)$ evolves under an external electromagnetic-type braking torque and an internal moment of inertia $I(\Omega;\,\mathrm{EOS},M,B,\ldots)$ that depends on rotation-induced stellar deformation, the (unknown) dense-matter equation of state, the stellar mass $M$, the composition of the core, and a time-dependent magnetic field $B(t)$. The angular momentum balance reads

$$ rac{d}{dt} igl(I(\Omega)\,\Omega igr) = -K(\Omega,B,	heta,\ldots)\,\Omega^{\,n_{m tor}},$$

where $K$ encodes the torque mechanism (e.g. magnetic dipole, plasma-filled magnetosphere, multipolar or wind torques, with inclination angle $	heta$ and possibly time-varying $B$), and $n_{m tor}$ is the intrinsic torque exponent. The observed braking index is defined by

$$n_{m obs} \;=\;  rac{\Omega\,\ddot\Omega}{\dot\Omega^{2}}.$$

Problem. Construct a single unified model of $\Omega(t)$ that simultaneously incorporates:
(i) low-frequency regimes where $n_{m obs}$ is governed by the torque law $K(\Omega,B,	heta,\ldots)$;
(ii) high-frequency regimes where deviations of $n_{m obs}$ from the torque-only value are dominated by the rotation dependence $dI/d\Omega$ arising from centrifugal deformation;
(iii) a family of physically admissible equations of state for neutron-star matter and the resulting mass dependence $I=I(\Omega;M,\mathrm{EOS})$;
(iv) uncertain interior composition (e.g. nucleonic, hyperonic, quark matter) and core microphysics (superfluidity, superconductivity, vortex pinning);
(v) magnetic-field evolution, including possible decay, growth, or geometric reorientation.

Determine, within this unified framework, the explicit functional form

$$n_{m obs} \;=\; \mathcal{F}\! igl(\Omega,\dot\Omega,\,I,I',I'';\,K,\partial_\Omega K,\partial_B K;\,\dot B,\dot	heta,\ldots igr),$$

and identify which combinations of the underlying microphysical parameters (EOS stiffness, mass, composition indicators, magnetic-field decay timescale, torque exponents) are constrained by a measurement of $n_{m obs}$ for a given pulsar. Specifically, characterize the inverse problem: given measured values $\{\Omega,\dot\Omega,\ddot\Omega\}$ (and where available $\dddot\Omega$) for a population of isolated pulsars spanning a wide range of spin frequencies, determine the largest set of microphysical parameters that can be uniquely or jointly bounded, and exhibit the model that achieves this.

