Consider an obliquely rotating, magnetized neutron star: a perfectly conducting star rotating with angular velocity Ω about an axis that is inclined at a nonzero angle α to its magnetic dipole axis, embedded in a relativistic electron–positron (and possibly ion) plasma that fills its magnetosphere and outflows as a relativistic wind. The rotation generates an inductive electric field E_ind = −(Ω×r)×B/c (in the non-corotating, observer frame), and Maxwell's equations include the displacement current ∂E/∂t.

Construct a self-consistent global model — i.e., a solution of the coupled system consisting of Maxwell's equations and the relativistic plasma equations of motion (with appropriate kinetic or fluid closure including pair creation) — for the electromagnetic field (E, B) and the plasma (charge density ρ, current density J, particle distribution functions) throughout the entire pulsar magnetosphere and wind, such that the model:

(a) is globally consistent across the closed-field region, the open-field region, the light cylinder (radius r_L = c/Ω), and the asymptotic wind zone, without assuming rigid corotation of the plasma with the star;
(b) explicitly accounts for the inductive electric field and the displacement current;
(c) admits localized regions ("gaps") in which the component of E parallel to B is nonzero (E_∥ ≠ 0), allowing particle acceleration and pair production, while remaining consistent with the global field and current structure;
(d) determines the locations and properties of these accelerating regions, the global current circuit, and the spin-down torque, without relying on the approximations (force-free, ideal MHD, vacuum, or strict corotation) whose individual limitations preclude a complete description.

Provide such a global model, or prove that it exists and characterize it; in particular, identify the locations within the magnetosphere and wind where charged particles are accelerated.

