representations.normalized:
A pulsar is a rapidly rotating, strongly magnetized neutron star whose corotating magnetosphere is filled with a relativistic electron–positron (and possibly ion) plasma generated by pair cascades, and which emits highly polarized, extremely bright, narrow-band radio pulses whose brightness temperatures (often exceeding 10^25–10^30 K) require a coherent emission process rather than incoherent synchrotron-type radiation. Candidate coherent mechanisms that have been proposed include: (i) coherent curvature radiation by charged bunches or solitons moving along curved magnetic field lines; (ii) relativistic plasma maser/instability mechanisms (e.g., free-electron maser, anomalous-Doppler cyclotron-Cherenkov, or linear-acceleration emission); and (iii) various two-stream / parametric / turbulence-driven instabilities producing escaping electromagnetic modes.

Problem. Identify the specific coherent radio-emission mechanism that operates in pulsars and demonstrate that it is consistent, across the observed pulsar population (canonical pulsars, millisecond pulsars, magnetars, etc.), with all of the following simultaneously:
1. a physically motivated location of the source region within the magnetosphere (e.g., near the polar cap, in the outer gap, or at the light cylinder);
2. realistic local plasma parameters (multiplicity, Lorentz factor distribution, magnetic field strength, plasma frequency);
3. the inferred relativistic particle distribution functions, including any required beam/bunch/soliton structures and their stability;
4. the coherence requirements implied by the observed brightness temperatures;
5. the observed radio frequency range (roughly 100 MHz to a few GHz) and the form of the spectrum;
6. the observed polarization behavior, including the high degrees of linear polarization, orthogonal polarization modes, and characteristic position-angle swings.

Provide such a mechanism, with quantitative predictions derivable from first principles, or prove that no single mechanism among the existing candidates can simultaneously satisfy all of (1)–(6).

