Consider ultra-relativistic Pb–Pb collisions at LHC energies. Hadron yields measured at midrapidity are commonly described by a statistical (thermal) hadronization model in which the abundances of hadron species are fixed at a chemical freeze-out characterized by a temperature T_chem, a baryochemical potential μ_B, and a fireball volume V. The proton-to-pion ratio (p+p̄)/(π⁺+π⁻) measured at √s_NN = 2.76 TeV is found to lie significantly below the prediction obtained from a thermal fit using the value of T_chem extracted from pre-LHC data. Lowering T_chem by roughly 10 MeV brings the proton-to-pion ratio closer to the data, but creates tension with measured multi-strange hyperon abundances (Λ, Ξ, Ω), which favor the higher T_chem. Independently, ratios of short-lived resonances to their stable daughters (e.g. K*/K, ρ/π, Λ*/Λ) are well reproduced only by hadronic transport codes (such as EPOS or UrQMD) that include a finite hadronic rescattering phase between chemical and kinetic freeze-out, disfavoring single-temperature scenarios with T_chem = T_kin.

Identify and justify a physical mechanism, defined within a hydrodynamic-plus-hadronic-cascade framework, that simultaneously:
(a) reproduces the observed suppression of the (p+p̄)/(π⁺+π̄⁻) ratio at LHC;
(b) is consistent with the measured Λ, Ξ, and Ω hyperon yields without requiring a reduced T_chem;
(c) reproduces the measured short-lived resonance-to-stable ratios.

In particular, determine whether late baryon–antibaryon annihilation in the hadronic phase (equivalently, a late effective baryon freeze-out below T_chem driven by inelastic BB̄ → mesons reactions with insufficient back-reaction rate) can be uniquely distinguished from alternative explanations (e.g. flavor-dependent freeze-out, non-equilibrium pion chemical potentials, or modifications of the hadron mass spectrum) using currently available observables, or whether such alternatives remain phenomenologically degenerate.

