Consider the magnetic-field fluctuations of the turbulent interplanetary solar wind. Let $\mathbf{B}_0$ denote the local background magnetic field and write the total field as $\mathbf{B}(\mathbf{x},t)=\mathbf{B}_0+\mathbf{b}(\mathbf{x},t)$, where $\mathbf{b}$ is the fluctuating component with characteristic wave vector $\mathbf{k}$. Several idealized decompositions of $\mathbf{b}$ have been proposed:

(i) a "slab" component of planar, parallel-propagating Alfvén waves, for which $\mathbf{k}\parallel\mathbf{B}_0$ and $\mathbf{b}\perp\mathbf{B}_0$;
(ii) a "quasi-two-dimensional" component for which both $\mathbf{k}\perp\mathbf{B}_0$ and $\mathbf{b}\perp\mathbf{B}_0$;
(iii) a population of highly refracted MHD waves whose wave vectors have been substantially deflected from their source directions by inhomogeneities;
(iv) pressure-balanced structures, i.e., quasi-static fluctuations satisfying $\delta(p_{\text{thermal}}+|\mathbf{B}|^2/(8\pi))\approx 0$;
(v) any mixture of (i)–(iv).

Determine the three-dimensional symmetry of solar-wind magnetic-field turbulence: which of the above components, or which weighted combination of them, correctly describes the observed fluctuations, and in particular establish whether the perpendicular component is genuinely quasi-two-dimensional (with $\mathbf{k}$ and $\mathbf{b}$ both perpendicular to $\mathbf{B}_0$) as opposed to consisting of highly refracted waves or pressure-balanced structures.

