An additive cube is a nonempty finite word $w = w_1 w_2 w_3$ over a finite alphabet $A \subset \mathbb{Z}$ such that the three factors $w_1, w_2, w_3$ have equal length and equal digit sums (i.e., $\sum_{i} w_1[i] = \sum_i w_2[i] = \sum_i w_3[i]$, where the letters are interpreted as integers). An infinite word $\mathbf{w} \in A^{\mathbb{N}}$ is called additive-cube-free if no factor of $\mathbf{w}$ is an additive cube.

Determine, for each of the following alphabets, whether there exists an infinite additive-cube-free word over it:

(a) $A = \{0, 1, 2, 3\}$;
(b) $A = \{0, 1, 4\}$;
(c) $A = \{0, 2, 5\}$.

