Setup. In the Quantum Theory of Atoms in Molecules (QTAIM), a molecular system described by an electronic wavefunction Ψ and total electron density ρ(r) is partitioned into disjoint topological atoms Ω_A, where each Ω_A is a basin of the gradient vector field of ρ bounded by zero-flux interatomic surfaces S(Ω_A) satisfying ∇ρ(r)·n(r) = 0 for all r on S(Ω_A). Within each atomic basin one defines the kinetic energy T(Ω_A), the potential energy V(Ω_A), and the atomic energy E(Ω_A). At an equilibrium nuclear geometry the Hellmann–Feynman forces F_A on every nucleus vanish, and one has the atomic virial theorem 2T(Ω_A) + V(Ω_A) = 0, which permits the identification E(Ω_A) = −T(Ω_A) and yields a consistent additive partition of the molecular energy E = Σ_A E(Ω_A).

Problem. Construct an extension of the QTAIM subsystem virial and energy analysis to arbitrary (non-equilibrium) nuclear configurations at which the Hellmann–Feynman forces F_A on the nuclei are non-zero. Specifically, define for each topological atom Ω_A a kinetic-energy functional T(Ω_A), a potential-energy functional V(Ω_A) (incorporating contributions arising from the non-vanishing nuclear forces), and an atomic energy functional E(Ω_A) that:

(i) reduce to the standard QTAIM definitions when all F_A = 0;
(ii) satisfy a generalized atomic virial relation that consistently accounts for the non-zero forces on the nuclei contained in or bounding Ω_A;
(iii) give an additive decomposition E = Σ_A E(Ω_A) of the total electronic (or total molecular) energy at the chosen non-equilibrium geometry;
(iv) are invariant under origin translation and behave consistently along nuclear displacements such as vibrational distortions and reaction-path geometries.

Either provide such a construction together with proofs of (i)–(iv), or show that no construction satisfying (i)–(iv) can exist.

