Consider a sequential interaction between two agents, a learner $L$ and another agent $O$, taking place over discrete time steps $t = 1, 2, \dots$ in a stochastic environment specified by a state space $S$, joint action space $A_L \times A_O$, transition kernel $P(s' \mid s, a_L, a_O)$, and observation model from which $L$ observes a history $h_t = (s_1, a_{L,1}, a_{O,1}, \dots, s_t)$. The other agent $O$ chooses actions according to a behaviour policy $\pi_O^t : H \to \Delta(A_O)$ which is allowed to vary with $t$: for example, $\pi_O^t$ may be drawn from a finite or countable set of candidate policies $\{\pi^{(1)}, \pi^{(2)}, \dots\}$ with switches occurring at unknown times, or may evolve continuously through an adaptation/learning process unknown to $L$ (including the possibility that $O$ is itself a learner or a human whose strategy drifts).

The problem is to design, and rigorously analyze, a learning procedure for $L$ that simultaneously:
(i) identifies, at each time $t$, a model $\hat\pi_O^t$ of the current behaviour policy of $O$ from a hypothesis class $\Pi$ (which may itself need to be expanded online);
(ii) tracks changes in $\pi_O^t$ as they occur, detecting switches or drift with bounded delay;
(iii) predicts future actions $a_{O,t+1}, a_{O,t+2}, \dots$ with prediction error converging, in an appropriate sense, to that of an oracle that knows $\pi_O^t$;
under minimal stationarity assumptions on the sequence $(\pi_O^t)_{t \ge 1}$.

Formally: provide an algorithm together with a non-trivial regret or sample-complexity guarantee — for instance a bound on $\sum_{t=1}^T \mathbb{E}\,\mathrm{KL}\!\left(\pi_O^t(\cdot \mid h_t)\,\|\,\hat\pi_O^t(\cdot \mid h_t)\right)$ or on the cumulative loss of the resulting best response of $L$ — that holds whenever $(\pi_O^t)$ has bounded variation, a bounded number of switches, or arises from a known class of adaptive/learning dynamics, and which matches information-theoretic lower bounds for the corresponding non-stationary setting. Equivalently: characterize the classes of time-varying opponent behaviours for which such identification, tracking, and prediction are achievable, and exhibit matching upper and lower bounds.

