Consider the Einstein vacuum equations Ric(g) = 0 on a 4-dimensional Lorentzian manifold (M, g). For parameters (m, a) with 0 ≤ |a| < m, the Kerr family g_{m,a} provides an explicit two-parameter family of stationary, axisymmetric, asymptotically flat solutions describing rotating black holes; the Schwarzschild solutions correspond to a = 0.

Given a vacuum initial data set (Σ, h, k) — that is, a 3-manifold Σ with a Riemannian metric h and a symmetric 2-tensor k satisfying the Einstein constraint equations — its maximal globally hyperbolic future development under the Einstein vacuum equations is uniquely determined.

Question: Prove that the Kerr family is nonlinearly asymptotically stable as a solution of the Einstein vacuum equations. More precisely, show that for every subextremal Kerr parameter pair (m_0, a_0) with |a_0| < m_0, there exists ε > 0 such that for any asymptotically flat vacuum initial data set (Σ, h, k) which is ε-close (in a suitable weighted Sobolev norm) to the induced data of Kerr (m_0, a_0) on a Cauchy hypersurface, the maximal future development:
(i) possesses a complete future null infinity;
(ii) admits a domain of outer communication that is bounded in the future by a smooth event horizon;
(iii) asymptotically settles down, in the exterior region, to a member g_{m_∞, a_∞} of the Kerr family with parameters (m_∞, a_∞) close to (m_0, a_0), with quantitative decay rates of the perturbation toward this final Kerr metric.

