Consider a four-parameter family of polynomials $\{Q_n(z;\mu,\nu,\alpha,\theta)\}_{n\ge 0}$ in the real variable $z$, taken to be orthogonal on (a subset of) the real line, together with a companion four-parameter family $\{G_n(z;\mu,\nu,\alpha,\beta)\}_{n\ge 0}$ which is a discrete-variable version of the same class. Both families are specified solely by a three-term recursion relation of the form
$$z\,P_n(z) = a_n(\text{params})\,P_{n+1}(z) + b_n(\text{params})\,P_n(z) + c_n(\text{params})\,P_{n-1}(z),$$
together with the initial conditions $P_{-1}\equiv 0$ and $P_0\equiv 1$, where the coefficients $a_n,b_n,c_n$ are explicit rational functions of $n$ and the four parameters that produce a positive-definite Jacobi operator on the appropriate parameter domain.

Determine, in closed analytic form, the following objects associated with each of the families $Q_n$ and $G_n$:

(a) the orthogonality measure (weight function) on the real line, including its support and normalization;
(b) a generating function $\sum_{n\ge 0} Q_n(z;\mu,\nu,\alpha,\theta)\,t^n$ (and analogously for $G_n$);
(c) the large-$n$ asymptotic behavior of $Q_n(z;\mu,\nu,\alpha,\theta)$ and $G_n(z;\mu,\nu,\alpha,\beta)$, both inside and outside the spectrum;
(d) the explicit orthogonality relation, including the squared-norm formula;
(e) a Rodrigues-type formula expressing the polynomials via repeated differentiation (or differencing) of the weight;
(f) forward and backward shift operator relations relating the polynomial of degree $n$ to that of degree $n\pm 1$ with shifted parameters;
(g) the location and asymptotic distribution of the zeros;
(h) a closed-form representation, ideally as a terminating generalized hypergeometric function ${}_pF_q$ (or a basic hypergeometric ${}_p\phi_q$ in the discrete case), expressing $Q_n$ and $G_n$ explicitly in $n$ and the parameters.

