In semiclassical gravity, a black hole formed from gravitational collapse of matter in a pure quantum state is predicted by Hawking's calculation to evaporate by emitting thermal (mixed) radiation, eventually disappearing. This appears to map an initial pure state to a final mixed state, contradicting the unitary time evolution required by quantum mechanics, and seemingly destroying information about the initial state.

Provide a precise theoretical resolution of this paradox. Specifically, formulate a consistent framework that describes the entire process of black-hole formation and complete evaporation in such a way that:

(i) the global evolution from the initial infalling matter state to the final outgoing radiation state is unitary, so that pure states evolve to pure states;
(ii) all information about the initial state is in principle recoverable from the final state (no genuine loss of information);
(iii) the framework reproduces, to leading order in the appropriate semiclassical limit, Hawking's prediction of approximately thermal radiation at the Hawking temperature for black holes far from the endpoint of evaporation;
(iv) the framework is consistent with the equivalence principle for an infalling observer (i.e. no drama at the horizon for a sufficiently large black hole) or, alternatively, gives a principled explanation of which classical expectation must be modified and why.

Identify the underlying mechanism (e.g. subtle correlations in the Hawking radiation, non-locality, modifications to the near-horizon geometry, holographic encoding, or otherwise) by which information is preserved, and demonstrate mathematically that the proposed mechanism is compatible with quantum mechanics, general relativity in its domain of validity, and the observed entanglement-entropy behavior (Page curve) of the radiation.

