Let a system of differential equations (either ordinary or partial) be given, and let G be a Lie (point, contact, generalized, or nonclassical) symmetry group admitted by the system, where nonclassical symmetries are understood in the sense of Bluman–Cole: transformations leaving invariant the joint system consisting of the original equations together with the invariant surface conditions associated with the candidate vector fields. The task is to design, analyze, and further develop numerical discretization schemes (finite difference, finite element, finite volume, spectral, mesh-adaptive, or moving-frame based) on discrete grids such that the resulting discrete equations admit a discrete analogue of the symmetry group G acting on the lattice and dependent variables, in such a way that:

(i) every continuous symmetry (including nonclassical ones) of the differential system is inherited by the discrete scheme as an exact symmetry of the corresponding difference equations on the (possibly transformed) mesh;
(ii) the scheme is consistent with the differential system and convergent, with stability and accuracy properties at least comparable to standard non-invariant schemes;
(iii) group-invariant solutions, similarity reductions, and conservation laws stemming from the symmetries (including those obtained via nonclassical reductions) are reproduced exactly, or to higher order, by the numerical solutions.

Provide constructive algorithms achieving (i)–(iii), particularly for partial differential equations and for nonclassical symmetries, together with a convergence and error analysis framework that exploits the inherited symmetry structure.

