Consider model atmospheres of early B-type and late O-type stars (effective temperatures roughly 20{,}000–35{,}000 K), in which the populations of ionic species are computed from the radiative-transfer equation coupled with statistical-equilibrium (non-LTE) rate equations. Two classes of models are commonly used:

(a) plane-parallel hydrostatic models, in which the atmosphere is treated as a one-dimensional layered slab in hydrostatic equilibrium, neglecting stellar wind and magnetic fields;

(b) extended spherical models, which incorporate spherical geometry, a hydrodynamic stellar wind (mass loss producing a velocity field v(r)), and, where present, a stellar magnetic field B affecting level populations and line transfer.

Two independent procedures are used to determine the effective temperature T_eff and surface gravity log g:

(i) the ionization-balance method, which fits T_eff and log g by requiring that abundances derived from successive ionization stages of C, N, and O (in particular, lines of C II/C III, N II/N III, O II/O III) agree;

(ii) the photometric / Balmer-line method, which fits T_eff and log g from broad-band photometric indices and the wings of hydrogen Balmer lines.

In observed early B and late O stars, method (i) systematically yields different values of (T_eff, log g) than method (ii), and the C II, N II, O II lines exhibit "superionization" — populations of these singly ionized species are lower than predicted by the plane-parallel non-LTE models, distorting the inferred abundances of carbon, nitrogen, and oxygen.

Problem. Construct a self-consistent atmospheric model for early B-type and late O-type stars that:

1. quantitatively reproduces the observed superionization of C II, N II, and O II;

2. eliminates the systematic discrepancy between (T_eff, log g) values determined by the ionization-balance method (i) and by the photometric / Balmer-line method (ii);

3. yields, simultaneously and consistently, reliable photospheric abundances of C, N, and O.

Specify the geometric assumptions, the wind hydrodynamics (mass-loss rate, velocity law), the treatment of the magnetic field, and the non-LTE radiative-transfer scheme required for such a model, and demonstrate that the resulting line spectrum and continuum are consistent with observations across this temperature range.

representations.original:
Consider model atmospheres of early B-type and late O-type stars (effective temperatures roughly 20{,}000–35{,}000 K), in which the populations of ionic species are computed from the radiative-transfer equation coupled with statistical-equilibrium (non-LTE) rate equations. Two classes of models are commonly used:

(a) plane-parallel hydrostatic models, in which the atmosphere is treated as a one-dimensional layered slab in hydrostatic equilibrium, neglecting stellar wind and magnetic fields;

(b) extended spherical models, which incorporate spherical geometry, a hydrodynamic stellar wind (mass loss producing a velocity field v(r)), and, where present, a stellar magnetic field B affecting level populations and line transfer.

Two independent procedures are used to determine the effective temperature T_eff and surface gravity log g:

(i) the ionization-balance method, which fits T_eff and log g by requiring that abundances derived from successive ionization stages of C, N, and O (in particular, lines of C II/C III, N II/N III, O II/O III) agree;

(ii) the photometric / Balmer-line method, which fits T_eff and log g from broad-band photometric indices and the wings of hydrogen Balmer lines.

In observed early B and late O stars, method (i) systematically yields different values of (T_eff, log g) than method (ii), and the C II, N II, O II lines exhibit "superionization" — populations of these singly ionized species are lower than predicted by the plane-parallel non-LTE models, distorting the inferred abundances of carbon, nitrogen, and oxygen.

Problem. Construct a self-consistent atmospheric model for early B-type and late O-type stars that:

1. quantitatively reproduces the observed superionization of C II, N II, and O II;

2. eliminates the systematic discrepancy between (T_eff, log g) values determined by the ionization-balance method (i) and by the photometric / Balmer-line method (ii);

3. yields, simultaneously and consistently, reliable photospheric abundances of C, N, and O.

Specify the geometric assumptions, the wind hydrodynamics (mass-loss rate, velocity law), the treatment of the magnetic field, and the non-LTE radiative-transfer scheme required for such a model, and demonstrate that the resulting line spectrum and continuum are consistent with observations across this temperature range.

